      {
        "id": "S3.SS2.p5",
        "tag": "div",
        "class": "ltx_para",
        "start_line": 1521,
        "end_line": 1552,
        "raw_sha256": "34ba1bbefaee76005cec71b871140ba9441be55e84a39f32da386d748eb96602",
        "mathtext": "Having specified the dynamics, it remains to choose the integration\nhorizon. For fixed $(y,\\widetilde{x})$ , the deterministic part of\n(3.5) is harmonic motion about its equilibrium\nposition $c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ . In the absence of bounces, its position at\ntime $t$ satisfies\n\n$x_{t}-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}=(x_{0}-c_{y,\\widetilde{x}})\\cos t+\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,p_{0}\\sin t\\,.$\n\n(3.6)\n\nAs discussed earlier, we choose the integration horizon $t=\\pi$ , corresponding to one\nhalf-period. This is the first non-zero harmonic time at which the\nposition $x_{\\pi}$ no longer depends on the initial momentum $p_{0}$ .\nWithout bounces, the resulting map is\n\n$(x_{\\pi},p_{\\pi})=(2c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}-x_{0},-p_{0})\\,.$\n\n(3.7)\n\nThus, the position is reflected through the equilibrium position\n$c_{y,\\widetilde{x}}$ and retains its dependence on $x_{0}$ . Bounce\nevents correct for the residual force along the trajectory, while the\nfixed horizon $t=\\pi$ retains the memory-preserving structure of the\nhalf-turn process.\n",
        "disposition": "NODE",
        "classification": "selected-source-node",
        "reason": "Explicit harmonic motion is background; half-turn return/kernel is outside the selected finite prefix.",
